overall:
I checked all 150 rows against the glossary, and 16 have a problem. On the whole the sample follows the glossary closely: the formula order, numeral dates, village nominatives from §4.3/4.4, the fixed clergy forms, the Step-3 stems, Case-2 maiden names, the twins template and the placeholders are almost all applied correctly.

The violations that do occur fall into four groups:
- **Genitive -я after a consonant** (§7.2), misapplied five times: Szykinia, Lingia, Ramunia, Tania, Kazragi.
- **Other suffix slips:** two -ювна → -iuwna for -iowna (Balsiuwna, Buczniuwna), one -ова genitive written as \"-owa\" (Patwowa), and one wrongly undeclined -is stem (Abaris, should be Abarisa).
- **One-off rule breaks:** \"parafii Kule [?]\" (Kule takes no [?]), one godfather written two ways (Marcijan / Marcjan), and visitation notes and the previous entry's ending inside the fields of 0092_R3.
- **Formal gaps:** an entirely empty row (0004_R1) and missing final full stops (0169_L1).

The bigger issue is the glossary itself, not the translators. Its own templates produce Polish that a native reader will find wrong or odd:
- the parents field opens with a dangling genitive (\"Włościan, prawowitych małżonków …, córka\");
- the church phrase stacks three adjectives in front of the noun;
- \"Sakramentu\" is capitalised;
- twins are written \"niemowlęta bliźnięta\";
- \"-owna\" without ó, \"l\" for every л and \"rż\" all make ordinary surnames (Pawlowski, Kawnowna, Brżeziński, Szymkewicz) look misspelled;
- missing table entries turn Мацей into \"Macej\" and Кристина into \"Kristina\".

Because every entry follows these rules, fixing them in the glossary (mechanically, as its header proposes) would improve the Polish far more than any of the per-row corrections.
